  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 109 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThe 500 mb trough currently over the southwestern CONUS eventually \nlifts over the midwest Monday night into Tuesday, with the resultant \nsurface low generally passing over eastern Nebraska and South \nDakota. Increased theta-e advection in the warm sector of this low \nwill bring the potential for light rain to portions of central Iowa \non Tuesday, although dry air intrusion from the southeast and slight \ndisplacement from the main synoptic lift will likely limit \nprecipitation coverage. A southwest flow pattern generally dominates \nthe central US through the week, maintaining a steady stream of \nmoisture and warmer, southerly air. As waves eject through this \npattern, we'll see additional rain chances throughout the remainder \nof the forecast. Some of these rain chances look to also have some \nweak instability associated with them, suggesting the potential for \na few thunderstorms as well. The pattern remains relatively active \ninto next weekend, so will continue to monitor any rain and \nthunderstorm potential as we progress through the week.",
